1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,380
Host: Welcome back to Field Notes, the show about the people who grow our food.

2
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:12,780
Today I'm sitting down with Dana Whitfield, who runs a fourth-generation cattle ranch outside Billings, Montana.

3
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:15,720
Dana, thanks for having us out here.

4
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:21,770
Dana: Thanks for coming. You picked a good week, the grass is finally green.

5
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:28,170
Most mornings start before dawn. I'm out feeding the cattle around five, while it's still cold.

6
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:32,650
We run about four hundred head on roughly six thousand acres.

7
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:39,050
My great-grandfather homesteaded this place in 1911, and he built that red barn you drove past.

8
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:46,220
Back then everything was done by horse. Now my son runs the tractor and I fix the fences.

9
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:51,090
It's a lot of the same work, honestly, just with better coffee.

10
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:58,290
Host: Let's talk about the last few years. Things have been hard for a lot of ranchers.

11
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:03,960
Dana: They have. Feed costs went up about forty percent in two years.

12
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:10,750
When the drought hit in 2021, the creek that runs through our lower pasture dried up completely.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,380
We had to haul water in by truck every single day for three months.

14
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:24,710
A lot of our neighbors sold their herds that year. Some of them had been here as long as we have.

15
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:30,340
You drive into town now and half the stores on Main Street are empty.

16
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:36,360
That's the part people don't see. It's not just one farm, it's the whole community.

17
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:41,640
Host: So what changed for you? How did you make it through?

18
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:45,770
Dana: Honestly, we started paying attention to the soil.

19
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:52,170
There's a whole world underground, the microbiome, the bacteria and fungal networks that feed the roots.

20
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:58,190
If you keep that alive, the grass holds water longer, even in a dry summer.

21
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:05,360
So we started moving the cattle every few days instead of leaving them in one field all season.

22
00:02:05,610 --> 00:02:12,150
We call it rotational grazing. The herd eats, tramples, fertilizes, and then the land gets to rest.

23
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:20,480
The first year I wasn't sure. By the third year, our grass was coming back thicker than I'd ever seen it.

24
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:23,810
We cut our hay purchases almost in half.

25
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:27,550
Host: That's remarkable. Is technology part of that?

26
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:34,750
Dana: Some. We put sensors in the water tanks so I can check levels from my phone.

27
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,080
And my daughter flies a drone over the far pasture to count cattle, which saves me half a day on horseback.

28
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:47,950
But most of it is just walking the land and paying attention.

29
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,850
Host: What does a good day look like for you now?

30
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:02,750
Dana: A good day is when the calves are healthy, the rain comes when it should, and we all sit down for dinner together.

31
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,770
My kids grew up here. My daughter is studying agriculture at Montana State in Bozeman.

32
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:14,790
She's already telling me what I'm doing wrong, which I take as a good sign.

33
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:23,500
Every spring we have about a hundred and fifty calves born, and I still go out at night to check on them.

34
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:29,900
There's nothing like watching a newborn calf stand up for the first time in the snow.

35
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:32,840
It reminds you why you do this.

36
00:03:33,890 --> 00:03:40,810
Host: Last question. What do you want people in the city to understand about where their food comes from?

37
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:47,240
Dana: That it comes from families. Real people getting up before sunrise in the cold.

38
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:53,260
When you buy beef at the grocery store, there's a ranch like ours behind it.

39
00:03:53,510 --> 00:04:00,050
Come out and visit a farmers market. Shake the hand of the person who grew your food.

40
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:04,920
That's how we keep places like this alive for another hundred years.

41
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:08,280
Host: Dana Whitfield, thank you so much.

42
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:13,530
That's it for this episode of Field Notes. We'll see you next week.
